# Plotting graphs and data visualization

**Authors:** Daniel H. Baker

## Abstract
<p>This chapter highlights the importance of displaying data in a clear and interpretable way for the purpose of scientific communication. It tackles some principles of data visualization that involve different conditions, axes, variability, and data. The <italic>ggplot2</italic> package is recognized as the most popular plotting package in <italic>R</italic>, which is part of the <italic>tidyverse</italic> suite of packages created by the makers of <italic>RStudio</italic>. The <italic>ggplot2</italic> functions use a plotting grammar, whereby a plot is constructed by adding layers of components. The chapter then discusses how to export graphs, and also how to combine several graphs into a multipart figure within <italic>R</italic> and without the need for additional software. It explains the considerations of atypical colour vision and uniformity when choosing a colour palette for plotting.</p>

**Source:** https://doi.org/10.1093/hesc/9780192896599.003.0018

